      "content": "While describing the control group as having higher variance is scientifically plausible for neurological populations, explicitly framing the specific outliers as \"just noise\" could effectively be challenged by the board as data manipulation if their scores are so close to the treatment group's results. The most robust defense would be to demonstrate that these scores, while unusual, still fall within the theoretical distribution of a healthy control group without contradicting other independent metrics or known study protocols. Presenting these subjects as part of a wider or suspected variability spectrum is often more credible than dismissing them entirely, but you must ensure this interpretation is fully supported by the broader statistical model and not just based on these few data points. Ultimately, the safest approach for your board presentation is to formally inspect them rather than ignore them, showing the board that you have acknowledged and understood the full picture of your trial data.",
